<% content_for :title, "Buddhism & Bipolar | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="buddhism-bipolar-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="buddhism-bipolar-hero">

    <div class="buddhism-bipolar-hero-content">

      <p class="buddhism-bipolar-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PERSPECTIVES / BUDDHISM
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BIPOLAR</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>BUDDHIST LENS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="buddhism-bipolar-intro">
        YOU ARE EXPERIENCING THE STORM.<br>
        YOU ARE NOT THE FUCKING STORM.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 01 / SUFFERING -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-pink">

    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">

      <p class="buddhism-number">
        01 / THE BASIC IDEA
      </p>

      <h2>
        START WITH<br>
        SUFFERING.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">

      <p class="buddhism-lead">
        Buddhism doesn't promise a life without pain.
        It asks us to understand our relationship with pain.
      </p>

      <p>
        Bipolar disorder can bring experiences we didn't ask for:
        depression, mania, agitation, fear, impulsivity, anger,
        exhaustion, instability, and uncertainty.
      </p>

      <p>
        Buddhism doesn't require pretending those experiences
        aren't painful.
      </p>

      <p>
        Instead, it asks another question:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        What happens when I stop fighting the fact that this
        moment is already happening?
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Acceptance doesn't mean liking it. It doesn't mean giving up.
        And it doesn't mean refusing treatment.
      </p>

      <p>
        It means starting with reality instead of spending all of
        our energy demanding that reality be different before we
        decide what to do next.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 02 / IMPERMANENCE -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-yellow">

    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">

      <p class="buddhism-number">
        02 / IMPERMANENCE
      </p>

      <h2>
        THIS WILL<br>
        CHANGE.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">

      <p class="buddhism-lead">
        One of Buddhism's central teachings is impermanence:
        conditions change.
      </p>

      <p>
        That matters when your brain is telling you that whatever
        you're experiencing right now is permanent.
      </p>

      <p>
        Depression can say:
        <strong>"I will always feel like this."</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        Mania can say:
        <strong>"I finally figured everything out."</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        Anger can say:
        <strong>"I will feel this way forever unless I do something right now."</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        Buddhism gives us a quieter answer:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        This is here right now.<br>
        And this will change.
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Feelings change. Thoughts change. Energy changes.
        Episodes change. Circumstances change.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don't have to make a permanent decision because of
        a temporary mental state.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 03 / MINDFULNESS -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-black">

    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">

      <p class="buddhism-number">
        03 / MINDFULNESS
      </p>

      <h2>
        NOTICE<br>
        THE MIND.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">

      <p class="buddhism-lead">
        A thought appearing in your head does not automatically
        make that thought true.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mindfulness creates a little space between
        <strong>having an experience</strong> and
        <strong>reacting to the experience.</strong>
      </p>

      <div class="buddhism-example">

        <p>Instead of:</p>

        <h3>
          "I'M FUCKING FURIOUS."
        </h3>

        <p>Try noticing:</p>

        <h3>
          "ANGER IS HERE."
        </h3>

      </div>

      <p>
        Instead of immediately following:
        <strong>"This is an incredible idea."</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        You can notice:
        <strong>"My mind is producing a lot of exciting ideas right now."</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        That tiny linguistic difference can create room to observe
        what's happening before deciding what to do about it.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 04 / ATTACHMENT -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-purple">

    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">

      <p class="buddhism-number">
        04 / ATTACHMENT
      </p>

      <h2>
        DON'T<br>
        CLING.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">

      <p class="buddhism-lead">
        We don't only cling to things we like.
        We cling to identities, explanations, emotions,
        expectations, and versions of ourselves.
      </p>

      <p>
        Bipolar can make this complicated.
      </p>

      <p>
        We might cling to the energy of mania.
        We might cling to shame about things we've done.
        We might cling to the idea of who we were before diagnosis.
        We might even cling to the diagnosis itself.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        Bipolar may describe something happening in your mind.
        It does not have to become the entirety of who you are.
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Buddhism invites us to hold identity a little more loosely.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can acknowledge bipolar disorder without reducing
        yourself to bipolar disorder.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 05 / COMPASSION -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-pink">

    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">

      <p class="buddhism-number">
        05 / COMPASSION
      </p>

      <h2>
        DON'T ADD<br>
        ANOTHER ASSHOLE.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">

      <p class="buddhism-lead">
        Living with a difficult brain is already hard enough.
        You don't also need to spend every waking moment beating
        the shit out of yourself for having one.
      </p>

      <p>
        Compassion isn't letting yourself off the hook.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can take responsibility for something you did
        while manic, depressed, angry, impulsive, or unstable
        without deciding that the behavior proves you are
        fundamentally terrible.
      </p>

      <p>
        Responsibility asks:
        <strong>"What do I need to repair?"</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        Shame says:
        <strong>"I am beyond repair."</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        Those are not the same thing.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        Compassion gives you somewhere to stand while you change.
      </blockquote>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 06 / MIDDLE WAY -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-yellow">

    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">

      <p class="buddhism-number">
        06 / THE MIDDLE WAY
      </p>

      <h2>
        NOT EVERY<br>
        EXTREME.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">

      <p class="buddhism-lead">
        Buddhism repeatedly returns to a Middle Way:
        not collapsing into extremes.
      </p>

      <p>
        That's an interesting philosophy to bring to a disorder
        that can make life feel profoundly polarized.
      </p>

      <div class="buddhism-contrast">

        <p>EVERYTHING IS AMAZING.</p>

        <span>↕</span>

        <p>EVERYTHING IS FUCKED.</p>

        <span>↕</span>

        <p>
          MAYBE I DON'T HAVE TO BELIEVE EITHER EXTREME.
        </p>

      </div>

      <p>
        The Middle Way doesn't mean forcing yourself into emotional
        neutrality.
      </p>

      <p>
        It means recognizing that reality is often much larger
        than whatever extreme your mind is presenting right now.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 07 / PRACTICE -->
  <section class="buddhism-section buddhism-black">

    <div class="buddhism-section-heading">

      <p class="buddhism-number">
        07 / PUT IT INTO PRACTICE
      </p>

      <h2>
        OKAY.<br>
        NOW WHAT?
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-copy">

      <p class="buddhism-lead">
        You don't have to become a Buddhist to borrow something
        useful from Buddhism.
      </p>

      <div class="buddhism-practice-list">

        <div>
          <span>01</span>
          <p>
            <strong>NOTICE.</strong><br>
            What is happening in my mind and body right now?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>02</span>
          <p>
            <strong>NAME IT.</strong><br>
            Anger is here. Fear is here. Energy is high.
            Depression is here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>03</span>
          <p>
            <strong>REMEMBER IMPERMANENCE.</strong><br>
            This state will change.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>04</span>
          <p>
            <strong>DON'T IMMEDIATELY OBEY IT.</strong><br>
            A feeling can exist without becoming an action.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>05</span>
          <p>
            <strong>ADD COMPASSION.</strong><br>
            What would responding without attacking myself look like?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>06</span>
          <p>
            <strong>THEN CHOOSE.</strong><br>
            What action causes the least unnecessary suffering
            for myself and everyone involved?
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="buddhism-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="buddhism-ending-small">
        THE POINT ISN'T
      </p>

      <h2>
        NEVER FEEL<br>
        <span>THE STORM.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="buddhism-ending-copy">

      <p>
        The practice is learning to recognize the storm
        while it's happening.
      </p>

      <p>
        To stop confusing every thought with truth.
      </p>

      <p>
        To stop confusing every feeling with an instruction.
      </p>

      <p>
        And to remember that whatever is happening inside you
        right now is not the entirety of you.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
          bipolar_perspectives_path,
          class: "buddhism-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>